From 3f97b94ddd518ee6809f0e127f535836a5ea5dce Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Dylan Ironside <dylan@dylanironside.io>
Date: Sun, 15 Nov 2026 08:19:15 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 21/24] omarchy-mac: give Apple Silicon power profiles the CPU
 frequency floor

omarchy-power-profiles-daemon keeps Apple Silicon on schedutil in every profile, as it does Snapdragon X, and the energy-aware scheduler starts short-lived threads on the efficiency cores, so single-thread work loses most of the performance cores' speed. The runtime's omarchy-powerprofiles-floor (omacom/omarchy#18745) already fixes this on Snapdragon with a utilisation clamp floor that follows the profile; its unit is installed by a Qualcomm hardware leaf. Apple Silicon's own setup ships here, so the package carries the unit and its setup instead of a leaf under the runtime's install/hardware/apple/, which holds Intel Mac quirks (this replaces omacom/omarchy#18946).

Measured on a 14" M4 MacBook Pro (J604), AC, profile performance, one-thread STREAM-style copy and triad: no floor 47.3 and 53.5 GB/s, floor 768 67.8 and 85.5 GB/s, floor 1024 69.0 and 94.5 GB/s. Idle system power went from 4.99 W to 5.04 W with the 768 floor.

omarchy-mac-powerprofiles-floor.service runs the runtime's command, with the same sandbox as the runtime's unit, a platform ExecCondition like every other package service, and a ConditionPathExists on the command so a runtime without it skips the unit instead of failing it. The unit name differs from the runtime's so neither shadows the other. omarchy-mac-setup-system enables it once and records that with a marker written only after the enable succeeds, so a failed enable is retried on the next run and a later disable or mask stays the administrator's choice; on a runtime without the command it defers. On a live system it starts the unit with --no-block, which also covers an image's first boot, whose start jobs are already queued.

powerprofiles-floor-test stages the package and covers the deferral, the enable, the retry after a failed enable, a disable and a mask that survive, another platform, and systemd-analyze verify. The README and the manual's hardware page say what the floor does and how a Mac installed earlier gets it.

Signed-off-by: Dylan Ironside <dylan@dylanironside.io>
